# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Generated experimental output
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DECISION AND JUSTIFICATION -- read this before changing it.
#
# Two categories of generated file are treated differently, on purpose.
#
# EXCLUDED: data/sensor_messages.csv and results/raw_results.csv.
#   Both are large (the raw timing file reaches tens of megabytes on a full
#   run) and both are exactly reproducible: the dataset from the seed in
#   src/config.py, the raw timings by re-running the benchmark. Git handles
#   large, frequently-rewritten CSVs badly, and a repository whose history is
#   dominated by regenerable data is hard to review.
#
# COMMITTED: summary_results.csv, security_results.csv, kat_results.csv,
#   overhead_results.csv, memory_results.csv, environment.json and every
#   figure in results/graphs/.
#   These are the evidence the report cites. They are small, they change
#   rarely, and committing them means the repository at submission time
#   contains the exact numbers that appear in the write-up. An examiner can
#   verify a figure in the report against the file that produced it without
#   re-running anything, and environment.json records the machine those
#   numbers came from.
#
# If your supervisor prefers a fully self-contained archive, delete the two
# exclusion lines below and commit everything.
data/sensor_messages.csv
results/raw_results.csv

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Vendored third-party code
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EXCLUDED, with PROVENANCE.json committed instead.
#
# third_party/ holds code and test vectors copied from two upstream
# repositories (pyascon and ascon-c, both CC0-1.0). Re-distributing someone
# else's source inside a student repository is avoidable: PROVENANCE.json
# records the URL, the exact commit hash and the SHA-256 of every vendored
# file, which is strictly more useful for reproducibility than a copy with no
# version information. Anyone can restore the files with:
#     python scripts/setup_ascon.py
#
# If you would rather submit a single archive that runs with no network
# access, remove these three lines and commit third_party/ as well -- the CC0
# licence explicitly permits it.
third_party/pyascon/
third_party/ascon_kat/
!third_party/PROVENANCE.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Temporary clones left behind by a manual Ascon setup
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
temp_pyascon/
temp_asconc/


results/extended/raw_extended.csv
results/extended/latency_samples.csv